import nap3_ccra3_data

def by_topology:
  actions_by(
    "Typology",
    [ "No and low regret adaptation, including capacity building",
      "Climate-smart design, considering uncertainty",
      "Further investigation",
      "Early activities to support future adaptation",
      "Unclear typology noted in text" ]
  )

def by_aimed_at:
  actions_by(
    "Action aimed at",
    [ "Non-Gov",
      "Non-Government",
      "Government and/or ALB",
      "Unclear"
    ]
  )

MultiView([
  # Progress on Addressing CCRA3 Actions
  f"""
  When grouped by typology, most interventions fall under “No and low regret adaptation” or “Climate-smart
  design”, reflecting a pragmatic focus on cost-effective measures deliverable within the current policy cycle.
  Yet only a small minority are tagged as “Further investigation”, implying that the evidence base is already
  sufficient for most priority actions—an encouraging signal for implementation readiness. Interestingly, about
  half of the actions aimed at non-government actors (e.g., businesses) remain minimally addressed, suggesting
  that while government-led adaptation planning has advanced, diffusion of responsibility to the private sector
  and civil society continues to pose a major delivery challenge.
  """,
  # Patterns in Adaptation Typologies and Implementation Gaps
  f"""
  Across the 253 actions analysed, a clear skew emerges in how thoroughly NAP3 has addressed the CCRA3-identified
  needs. Roughly one in three actions are rated “Minimally addressed” or “Recognised but not addressed”, while
  fewer than one in five are classed as “Fully addressed.” This suggests that although progress has been made,
  a significant fraction of recommended interventions remain either at the planning stage or inadequately
  implemented. Actions associated with Chapter 4 (Infrastructure) and Chapter 6 (Business) risks appear
  over-represented among the minimally addressed group, hinting that adaptation in these cross-sectoral domains
  may lag behind more mature policy areas such as health or nature recovery.
  """
])
